---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import StandardDeviationCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/StandardDeviationCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="standard-deviation-calculator">
  <StandardDeviationCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the standard deviation calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste your numbers. Any separator works: commas, spaces, tabs or one value per line.</li>
      <li>Choose the sample formula (÷ n − 1) or the population formula (÷ n).</li>
      <li>Read the standard deviation, then check the deviation table and the step list to see how it was calculated.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The results grid always shows both versions side by side.
    </p>

    <h2>Sample or population?</h2>
    <p>
      This is the only choice you make, and it changes the result:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"></th>
            <th scope="col">Sample</th>
            <th scope="col">Population</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Symbol</td><td>s</td><td>σ (sigma)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Divide by</td><td>n − 1</td><td>n</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Use when</td><td>Your numbers are a subset of a bigger group</td><td>Your numbers are the entire group</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical case</td><td>A survey of 200 customers</td><td>The marks of all 30 pupils in one class, when that class is the whole question</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If you are unsure, use the sample formula. Almost all real data is a sample, and division by n − 1
      reports slightly more spread, which is the safer error.
    </p>

    <h2>Why n − 1? The Bessel correction</h2>
    <p>
      Deviations are measured from the sample mean, and the sample mean is the point that makes the sum of
      squared deviations as small as possible. Measured from the unknown true population mean, the squares
      would be larger. Division by n − 1 in place of n increases the result enough to cancel that bias, which
      gives an unbiased estimate of the population variance. With large n the difference is negligible; with
      n = 5 the variance differs by 12%.
    </p>

    <h2>The standard deviation formula, step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the mean: x̄ = Σx ÷ n.</li>
      <li>Subtract the mean from each value to get each deviation, x − x̄.</li>
      <li>Square each deviation, so that positive and negative distances both count.</li>
      <li>Add the squares: Σ(x − x̄)².</li>
      <li>Divide by n − 1 (sample) or n (population) to get the variance.</li>
      <li>Take the square root, which gives the answer in the original units.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The deviations always sum to zero. This is a useful check, and it is why step 3 exists: without
      squaring, the positive and negative deviations would cancel and every data set would have a spread of
      zero.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the extra statistics</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Variance is the standard deviation squared. It is in squared units (squared pounds, squared seconds), so reports use the square root.</li>
      <li>Standard error of the mean is s ÷ √n: how much the sample mean would change if you drew another sample of the same size. Four times the sample size halves it.</li>
      <li>Coefficient of variation is s ÷ |mean|, as a percentage. Use it to compare the spread of quantities measured in different units.</li>
      <li>Sum of squares is the raw Σ(x − x̄)² before division. Statistical software reports it often, and every variance formula divides it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the dot plot shows</h2>
    <p>
      Each value appears as a dot on a number line, with identical values stacked upward. The dashed line marks
      the mean and the shaded bands cover one and two standard deviations either side of it. For roughly
      bell-shaped data, expect about 68% of the dots inside the inner band and about 95% inside the outer one
      (the empirical rule). If the dots are lopsided or clustered at one end, the mean and standard deviation
      describe the data poorly, and the median with the interquartile range is a better summary.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9. The mean is 40 ÷ 8 = 5. The deviations are −3, −1, −1, −1, 0, 0, 2, 4 and
      their squares are 9, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 4, 16, summing to 32. The population variance is 32 ÷ 8 = 4, so
      σ = 2. The sample variance is 32 ÷ 7 ≈ 4.571, so s ≈ 2.138. Paste those eight numbers into the tool and
      the deviation table shows these rows.
    </p>

    <h2>Performance and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The tool computes the mean and sum of squares in a single pass, so it handles tens of thousands of values
      at once. The deviation table shows the first 200 rows to keep the page responsive; the totals row and
      every headline statistic cover the whole data set. Your data stays on your device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
